      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">SIN and banking</h3>
      <p>
        You need a Social Insurance Number to work and to receive government benefits. Apply through Service Canada online or at a Service Canada office with your passport and your work permit, study permit or confirmation of permanent residence. If you are a temporary resident, your SIN expires when your permit does. Major banks have newcomer accounts, and you can usually open one with your passport and immigration document even before you have a job. A secured credit card is a common way to start a Canadian credit history.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">MSP health coverage and the waiting period</h3>
      <p>
        B.C.'s Medical Services Plan (MSP) covers medically necessary doctor and hospital care. New residents wait for the rest of the month in which they establish residence plus two months, so someone who arrives on March 10 would expect coverage from June 1. The B.C. government says people new to Canada "should get private health care insurance while you wait." Permanent residents qualify if they make their home in B.C.; work and study permit holders qualify if their permit is valid for six months or more (working holiday permits included). Apply as soon as you arrive so your enrolment is processed before the waiting period ends.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">BC Services Card and driver's licence</h3>
      <p>
        Adults generally complete MSP enrolment by visiting an ICBC driver licensing office to have a photo taken for the BC Services Card, which can be combined with your driver's licence. ICBC says that after moving to B.C. you have 90 days to switch your valid licence. Licences from some countries can be exchanged without testing; for others you apply and also pass a knowledge test and a road test. To avoid B.C.'s Graduated Licensing Program you need proof of at least two years of driving experience, so bring a driving record or letter from your home licensing authority. Check ICBC's list of licence exchange jurisdictions before you arrive.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">School enrolment</h3>
      <p>
        Children of citizens and permanent residents attend public school free. Under the provincial criteria summarized by the Vancouver School Board, children of temporary residents are also generally eligible if a parent is authorized to work for a year or more and works at least 20 hours a week, or holds a qualifying study permit. Register through your school district's welcome or registration centre; bring passports, immigration documents and proof of address.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Free settlement services</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Before you arrive (approved PRs only):</strong> IRCC funds free online pre-arrival services for people outside Canada whose permanent residence has been approved, including Planning for Canada, Next Stop Canada, Settlement Online Pre-Arrival (SOPA) and Connexions Francophones. They are not available to people coming on temporary permits.</li>
        <li><strong>After you arrive (PRs):</strong> IRCC-funded agencies in Metro Vancouver include ISSofBC (Immigrant Services Society of BC), MOSAIC, S.U.C.C.E.S.S. and DIVERSEcity in Surrey. They help with job search, language classes, housing and paperwork. Since April 1, 2026, economic class immigrants can use federally funded settlement services for six years from the date they became permanent residents, falling to five years from April 1, 2027, so use them early.</li>
        <li><strong>Temporary workers, students and provincial nominees:</strong> B.C.'s own BC Newcomer Services Program serves work permit holders, some international students, provincial nominees waiting for a PR decision and naturalized citizens. Search for a provider on WelcomeBC.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="hard" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Is It Hard to Move to Vancouver?</h2>

      <p>
        Honestly, it is harder than it was a few years ago. Federal policy aims to bring temporary residents below 5% of Canada's population by the end of 2027, study and spousal permits have been narrowed, low-wage LMIAs are restricted in the Vancouver area, and the BC PNP rewards jobs outside the city. Rents have eased since 2023 but remain among Canada's highest. On the other hand, Vancouver has a large job market, established immigrant communities and free settlement help, and people in Care and Build occupations or with strong Express Entry profiles still have clear routes. Common mistakes to avoid:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Arriving as a visitor and hoping to find a job.</strong> Most work permits must be applied for properly, and visitor status cannot be used to work.</li>
        <li><strong>Choosing a job that cannot lead to PR.</strong> Check BC PNP eligibility and your realistic CRS score before you accept a role or move your family.</li>
        <li><strong>Skipping private insurance.</strong> A hospital visit during the MSP wait can be very expensive.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing the 90-day licence switch.</strong> ICBC gives new residents 90 days to switch to a B.C. licence, and the testing route can take longer to book than you expect.</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring the regions.</strong> If your field has employers in Victoria, Nanaimo or the Interior, compare those offers seriously.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Want a head start? Estimate your BC PNP registration score with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>, read <a href="/articles/how-long-to-get-pr-in-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how long it takes to get PR in Canada</a>, and if you already have a permit, see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-work-permit-guide" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">work permit guide</a> and the <a href="/articles/canada-temporary-resident-cap-2027-bc-work-permit-holders" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">2027 temporary resident cap explainer</a>.
      </p>

